  "summary": "Single-cell transcriptomics now pairs with multi-omics and machine learning to nominate cell subtypes and biomarkers in diverse diseased tissues.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is single-cell RNA sequencing applied to diseased tissue to resolve pathogenic cell states and their regulators. Several studies pair scRNA-seq with orthogonal layers: metabolomics in osteocyte-ablated liver steatosis, bulk transcriptomics with Scissor-CIBERSORTx in mouse dry eye, and LASSO/SVM-RFE feature selection in keratoconus. Other studies localize specific subpopulations, including SAA-expressing hepatocytes and their interaction with neutrophils in alcohol-related hepatitis, ferroptotic acinar cells engaging macrophages via TLR2 in pancreatitis, and two immunopathogenic ecotypes in autoimmune liver injury. The acetaminophen study adds spatial context, showing mid-zone hepatocytes suppress proliferation through an Atf4-Chop axis. The liver fibrosis review and the chicken thymus study extend this framing to transcription factor regulation and immunosuppressive microenvironments. What is new here is the pairing of cell-state resolution with candidate biomarker or regulator nomination.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-26"
}
